{
  "_note": "SCROLL-LINKED CUSTOM PROPERTIES — the substrate, and the reason U01 exists. The nearest scroll ancestor publishes its plane as CSS custom properties, computed by a PURE FUNCTION of one sample, so every renderer resolves them inside its own frame callback and NOTHING crosses the bus. That is the whole performance argument against a worklet: there is no second thread model because there is no second thread. TWO SPELLINGS PER LENGTH, and this is a CORRECTION to the U01 plan, whose example is not valid CSS. `--scroll-y` is UNITLESS points, so `calc(1 - var(--scroll-y) / 280)` types as a number; `--scroll-y-px` is the same value carrying `px`, so `translateY(calc(var(--scroll-y-px) * 0.5))` types as a length. The plan's `translateY(calc(var(--scroll-y) * 0.5))` with a unitless property produces a unitless length, which no CSS engine accepts, and `1 - <length>/280` is a unit mismatch. Both spellings are published for both axes. AXIS PLANES ARE INDEPENDENT: a scroll node contributes ONLY the plane of its own axis, so a horizontal rail inside a vertical page leaves `--scroll-y` to the page and owns `--scroll-x` itself, resolved from the NEAREST ancestor per axis. With no ancestor on an axis the keys are ABSENT rather than zero, so `var(--scroll-y, 0)` can tell 'no scroller' from 'at the top'. THE EVALUATOR is the native twin of what a browser does for free: substitute var() (with fallbacks, cycle-budgeted), then fold every calc() with CSS's own unit algebra — length+length only when the units match, zero being the one unitless length, at most one operand of a product carrying a unit, no division by a dimension. Anything it cannot type DROPS the declaration (invalid at computed value), which is what CSS itself does and what CssValue already does for a cyclic var(). Cases are three groups: publication, nested per-axis scope resolution, and evaluation.",
